Q: How to write a custom formatter for std::optional A: <p>One simple way to implement formatting codes (the <code>parse</code> function) is to <strong>inherit from a formatter</strong> that has the formatting codes you want to use.</p>
<p>Don't write <code>parse</code> at all.  Just use the inherited one.</p>
<pre><code>template &lt;typename T&gt;
struct fmt::formatter&lt;std::optional&lt;T&gt;&gt; : fmt::formatter&lt;T&gt;
{
  constexpr auto format (std::optional&lt;T&gt; const&amp; op, auto&amp; ctx) const 
  {
    if(op) return fmt::formatter&lt;T&gt;::format(op.value(), ctx);
    return format_to(ctx.out(), &quot;-&quot;);
  }
};

</code></pre>
<p>Now, if you want the <strong>vector's elements</strong> to be hex, you should use <code>&quot;{::x}&quot;</code>, because <code>&quot;{:x}&quot;</code> is asking <strong>the vector itself</strong> to be hex.</p>
<pre><code>    fmt::println(&quot;{::x}&quot;, vec);    
</code></pre>
